WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.433 --> 00:00:09.013
Leonor Michaelis ran routine tests for a Berlin city hospital by day and, by night,

2
00:00:09.013 --> 00:00:14.693
in "a laboratory of the most primitive kind," measured how light twists as sugar

3
00:00:14.693 --> 00:00:19.933
breaks apart a Jewish scientist who held the title of professor but not the salary,

4
00:00:20.113 --> 00:00:23.513
chair, or funding, for whom precision became a kind of shelter.

5
00:00:24.416 --> 00:00:27.836
With Maud Menten a Canadian who had already earned an M .D.

6
00:00:27.856 --> 00:00:29.136
and would earn a Ph .D.

7
00:00:29.136 --> 00:00:34.016
when most universities still refused women advanced degrees he saw why earlier

8
00:00:34.016 --> 00:00:38.956
enzyme experiments failed: they looked too late, after byproducts had tangled the

9
00:00:38.956 --> 00:00:39.956
reaction.

10
00:00:40.250 --> 00:00:44.530
So they started at the beginning, buffering the pH and measuring only the initial

11
00:00:44.530 --> 00:00:49.590
velocity. Menten's move was to chemically freeze the reaction at precise instants,

12
00:00:49.970 --> 00:00:53.230
stopping the slow optical drift that had masked the true rate.

13
00:00:54.033 --> 00:00:59.433
Their 1913 equation turned enzymology from a descriptive science into a quantitative

14
00:00:59.433 --> 00:01:02.333
one and they worked together for a single year.

15
00:01:03.133 --> 00:01:08.533
Menten went on to develop azo-dye stains still used in pathology today, climbed in

16
00:01:08.533 --> 00:01:13.253
the Canadian wilderness, drove a Model T through Pittsburgh in Paris hats, and was

17
00:01:13.253 --> 00:01:15.593
